# Performance and complexity

Every change is evaluated against real device cost: battery, memory, dropped frames, and
bundle size. Mobile hardware is the constraint, not the development machine.

## Mandatory analysis (explicit for hot paths)

1. **Algorithmic** — sort/filter/merge work is O(n log n) at worst; no O(n²) passes over
   user-visible lists; per-frame hot paths are O(1) or O(log n).
2. **Render** — unbounded lists use `FlatList` (or `FlashList`) with tuned `keyExtractor`,
   `windowSize`, and `getItemLayout` where possible; heavy children are memoized; store
   subscriptions use the smallest slice; styles live in `styled.ts` / `StyleSheet.create`,
   never inline object literals recreated per render.
3. **Animation** — animations run on the UI thread (Reanimated worklets); animation assets
   are sized appropriately; reduce-motion fallbacks exist.
4. **Bundle** — heavy dependencies are lazy-loaded; no barrel imports that drag entire
   packages into the initial bundle; every new dependency is justified in the PR.
5. **Lifecycle** — subscriptions, timers, and sockets are cleaned up on unmount; in-flight
   async work is aborted; ephemeral UI state is never persisted.

## Recording non-trivial costs

- PR description: expected input size, complexity class, and benchmarks when available.
- JSDoc complexity note on the affected symbol when the cost is non-obvious.
- A worst-case-input test in the colocated `*.test.ts`.

## Hot paths (extra scrutiny)

Feed/home screens, real-time views (chat, live data), large history lists, and anything
rendered during scroll or animation.

## Forbidden (review blockers)

- O(n²) loops over visible lists.
- Per-render `JSON.parse` / `JSON.stringify` on moderate state.
- Store selectors recreated inline in render.
- Synchronous heavy work during scroll or animation frames.
- Polling faster than the data actually changes.
- Eagerly loading heavy modules on first render when lazy loading suffices.
